      "text": "First, we relate $27 \\sqrt{3}$ to powers of 3 by examining $$27 \\sqrt{3}= 27 \\cdot 3^{\\frac{1}{2}}.$$ It is likely easier to work with combined factors, so this is becomes $$ \\begin{array}{rcl} 27 \\sqrt{3} &=& 3^3\\cdot 3^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\\\ &=& 3^{3+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ &=& 3^{\\frac{7}{2}} \\end{array} $$ Now we can apply the rules of logarithms to obtain \\[ \\begin{array}{rcl} \\log_3 27 \\sqrt{3} &=& \\log_3 3^{\\frac{7}{2}}\\\\ &=& \\frac{7}{2} \\end{array} \\] which is our final answer. \n\nAlternatively, we can write the expression out as $$\\log_3 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{3}.$$ We know that $2^{1/2}= \\sqrt{2}$ and so it is natural to rewrite this expression as $$\\log_3 3 \\cdot \\left( \\log_3 \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 2 \\right).$$ Additionally, we know that $2^{\\frac{1}{2}} = 2^{1/2} = \\sqrt{2}\\log_2 3$ and $\\log_3 3 = 1$, so this equals \\begin{align*}\n\\log_3 3 \\cdot \\log_3 \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 2 &= \\log_3 3 \\cdot \\left( \\log_3 3^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\right) \\cdot 2\\\\\n&= {\\left( 1 \\right) \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)}\\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\.\\,\\checkmark\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing this method asked more focus on the techniques or tricks required to solve the problem.",
